From bd3a84580c3bca020d2f0007cd52b006d3c9b211 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Matt Hargett <plaztiksyke@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 14 May 2026 21:29:33 -0700
Subject: pulley: add xfuncref_dispatch fused dispatch ops
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Phase 2 of the opcode-fusion track from PR #2: four new Pulley ops
(`xfuncref_dispatch_{x64, not_x64, x32, not_x32}`) that fuse the
call_indirect lazy-init brif with the two VMFuncRef field loads
(`wasm_call` + `vmctx`). Saves 2 match_loop dispatches per
call_indirect site vs the unfused sequence, vs phase 1's
`xband_s8_br_if_*` which saves 1.

Op semantics (forward form): if `src` is non-zero, load the wasm_call
code pointer from `src + offset_code` into `dst_code`, load the
callee vmctx pointer from `src + offset_vmctx` into `dst_vmctx`,
and conditionally branch by `offset`. The null side falls through
to the slow path (the lazy-init builtin). `src` is the
already-masked funcref pointer — the preceding `xband_s8 v, -2`
stays as a separate Pulley op upstream of the fused dispatch, since
its result is what feeds both the branch test here and (via
block-call-arg) the brif's continuation-block param.

The inverted form (`xfuncref_dispatch_not_*`) branches when src is
zero, for MachBuffer's branch-direction-flip fallthrough
optimization.

The 32-bit pointer-width variants cover arm64_32-apple-watchos
(`pulley32`) where VMFuncRef fields are 4-byte pointers and the
field offsets are 4 (`wasm_call`) and 12 (`vmctx`) instead of
8 and 24 on `pulley64`. Both fit in the i8 offset operand encoding.

Soundness: under the eager-init predicate
`is_eagerly_initialized_funcref_table`, the wasmtime runtime
populates every funcref slot at instance creation with a real tagged
pointer, so `src` is guaranteed non-null at runtime — the explicit
null check in each handler is defence-in-depth, matching the original
brif's role. It must fall through on null so the slow-path lazy-init
builtin stays callable in the (provably-unreachable) error case.

The handler's unaligned read is safe because (a) VMFuncRef has
pointer alignment by construction and (b) the offset is a compile-time
constant chosen from the canonical VMFuncRef layout.

See `docs/opcode-fusion-band-brif.md` (in the wasm-benchmark repo)
for the design discussion and the layered/calibration framing from
phase 1's measurement closeout.
